  "headline": "Fees must eventually fund both security and the storage/verification burden; today they fund neither fully. At ~$71,109/BTC the measured fee revenue is ~$1,456/block against a ~$198,549/block energy cost to produce a block and a ~$4,676/block modeled storage cost. On ONE fee basis (30-day mean) fees cover 0.7% of production and 31.1% of the modeled 10yr node-cost commitment (L_net = N*C*T). Note the two node-cost figures are the SAME cost at different horizons: one year of it reads 311.4% (x10 = the T-year figure), so the pair is one fact, not 'opex covered, permanence not'. Put plainly, fees fund roughly the first 3.1 years of the 10-year commitment; the remainder is carried by node operators. The subsidy (111.9% of energy cost) is still paying the security bill. Under a flat price the subsidy alone stops covering energy around 2028.",
